# Signed and packaged builds
web-ext-artifacts/

# Generated copies. Both were committed once - dist-chrome/ so a Chrome user could clone
# and "Load unpacked" without running anything, core/ because a .vsix may only contain
# files from inside vscode/. Between them that was 4,274 lines, just under half of all the
# JavaScript in the repository, and every source change carried a mechanical second and
# third copy of itself through every diff and every `git log -p`.
#
# They are built instead: browser/tools/build-chrome.sh and vscode/tools/sync-core.sh,
# both run automatically by the tests that check them, and by vscode:prepublish and the
# release workflow. Chrome users download the zip from a GitHub release.
browser/dist-chrome/
vscode/core/anchor.js
vscode/core/ollama.js
# ...but NOT core/package.json, which declares those copies as ESM and is hand-written.

# AMO / signing credentials - never commit these.
# The JWT issuer and secret from addons.mozilla.org belong outside the repository
# entirely; a sibling ../secrets/ directory, your shell profile or a password manager.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
secrets/
web-ext-config.local.*

# Node tooling lives at the repository root; browser/ and vscode/ stay free of it.
node_modules/
# package-lock.json IS committed: CI must install the exact versions that were tested,
# and "web-ext lint reports 0 warnings" is meaningless against a moving target.

# Editors and OS
# Not a blanket .vscode/ rule: vscode/.vscode/launch.json is the launch configuration
# that makes F5 work, and without it the extension appears to do nothing when you press
# it. That cost an afternoon once already.
/.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store

# Scratch
*.log

# VS Code extension packages
vscode/*.vsix
